<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friday Mosque of Herat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Mosque of Herat, Masjid-i Jami of Herat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Friday Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> is the principal congregational mosque of Herat in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. An earlier mosque on the site was rebuilt by the Ghurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> around 1200, and a Ghurid portal survives. It was repaired and redecorated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and later rulers, and its extensive tilework, much of it renewed in the twentieth century, makes it one of the city's landmarks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friday-mosque-of-herat.jpg" alt="Friday Mosque of Herat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: koldo hormaza from madrid, españa · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friday_Mosque_in_Herat,_Afghanistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
